{"kind":"report","text":"Decision on b315bb35 (planner, 2026-10-20). It replaces the single-anchor rule of bd3ace9f.\n\n1. One anchor cannot cover the closure. The archived journals keep copies of facts of two scopes that are not archived: the register and membership. One of these facts comes after the directory's genesis source (register entry 3). A hash chain proves only earlier entries. So without the register and membership journals, no proof from one anchor covers the closure. Those journals stay unexported (08e261c4, 3e2761e6).\n\n2. New contract. Offline replay trusts the head of each archived journal and an anchor list. The list names each register or membership fact that an archived journal keeps as a copy and that replay must prove: scope, incarnation, position and hash. Replay proves each such fact by its anchor. It proves no fact from a copied body, and it reads no register or membership journal. This widens the trust of offline replay from one fact to the listed facts. That is deliberate: it is the narrowest trust that meets offline replay while those two journals stay private.\n\n3. The list comes from the export. 9a builds it from the copies in the exported prefixes, ordered by scope and position. It names no fact of an archived journal, because replay must prove those facts by replay. Note that verify.ts:1020 checks anchors before replay, so an anchor for an archived journal would skip its replay. The list adds no export: each item is a fact whose copy the export already admits. Its length is at most the number of those copies in each pass, so the existing budgets bound it.\n\n4. Report. The replay report lists each anchor as taken on the caller's word (report.ts:39-40), with no change. The directory genesis source stays on the list. A caller ties a clone to the hosted demo by confirming that fact against the hosted register: seq 2, sha256:8682b3bb6c0dc2cc04a2e1ef65bb3e518657e6d20aa490b2c09b2d6ba9c97c5d. An authorized reader can confirm any other listed fact the same way. Replay reads nothing online.\n\n5. Everything else stays. A copy of any other scope that is not archived still refuses the export. Without its anchor, a fact stops replay with missing-dependency, and a wrong hash stops it with mismatch. The cohort lists, the membership kind list and the no-secret check do not change.\n\n6. Witnesses on the test host. The five fixture journals with the derived list replay to consistent, and the report lists exactly the register and membership anchors. Without one anchor, replay stops with missing-dependency at the first entry that uses that fact. With one hash changed, it stops with mismatch. The derived list names no archived scope. If replay then stops for another reason, report it to the planner before you change the contract.\n\n7. Slice 9 publishes the list with each archive pass, where a clone can read it. Its place is a matter for slice 9's review, inside the per-pass budget. The /grow/ page says that offline replay takes these facts on trust.\n\nNo deployment and no hosted read.","body":{"to":"builder"}}